Description: Screenshot tool with annotation, straight to the clipboard
 SnapCtrlAlt brings the QQ-screenshot style workflow to Linux desktops:
 press a global hotkey, drag to select a region, annotate it, and the result
 lands in the clipboard.
 .
  * global hotkey (Ctrl+Alt+D by default), tray resident;
  * selection with eight handles, moving, arrow-key nudging, and a full screen
    selection on double click;
  * an 8x magnifier with crosshair, coordinates and the #RRGGBB value while
    selecting;
  * annotation tools: rectangle, ellipse, arrow, pen, highlighter, text,
    numbering, mosaic, Gaussian blur and a colour picker, plus undo, redo,
    clear, save as, pin-to-desktop, cancel and finish;
  * a pinned screenshot window that stays on top for reference;
  * one-shot mode (--once) for desktop environment shortcuts and a built in
    self test (--selftest).
 .
 It is a Linux port of the Windows SnapCtrlAlt project.  The overlay is drawn
 with GTK3 and Cairo and captures the screen through X11 (Xlib), falling back
 to GDK and then to the XDG Desktop Portal on pure Wayland sessions.
